  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a constructive approach based on mathematical fairness principles and randomized search. The core idea is to explicitly satisfy the maximum lag penalty as a hard constraint during the optimization process. By targeting fairness across all lags, we enforce more balanced optimization, avoiding the tendency of previous algorithms to overfit specific lags. This approach ensures that no lag dominates the objective function, leading to more uniformly low overlaps. The method combines:\n- Lag-based multi-objective optimization\n- Progressive resolution refinement from 280 to 560 points\n- Consistent repair mechanisms to satisfy integral and box constraints\n- Hard constraints to limit maximum lag penalties early in optimization\n\nThe algorithm's key innovation is its Lag-weighted constraint system that translates the objective \"max_k integral \u03bb_k\" into enforceable maximum overlap constraints for each lag. We parameterize fair distribution by defining minimum acceptable overlap fraction for each lag, enforced through penalty functions. This approach directly addresses the bottleneck of local optima by maintaining a frontier of solutions across all lag ranges, preventing premature convergence to pathologies in high-traffic lags.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Discrete optimization domain: n-point samples over [0,2] with unit integral constraint\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, continuous over n-1 dimensions\n- Constraints:\n  1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  2. \u03a3h[i] = n/2\n  3. For each lag k: max_correlation[k] * dx \u2264 C (current_best_bound)\n- Objective: minimize the maximum correlation over all lags k \u2208 {-n+2, -n+3, ..., n-2}\n- Feasibility check: Repair candidate to enforce constraints through iterative midpoint projection\n\nThe algorithm defines three stages:\n\nA. Initialization and hard-constraint enforcement:\n   For initial_h_values at resolution n=560:\n    - \u03a3 = min(overlap_penalty_max, target_region)\n    - h[i] = clip(initial_h_values[i], 0, 1)\n    - Apply penalty field P[i] = sumk correction[k] * l2(overlap)\n    - Optimization seeks to minimize: -\u03a3P[k] + \u03bb_max_region\n\nB. Progressive lag weighting:\n   - Phased resolution: start at 560 points\n   - Weighting scheme: lag frequencies normalize P[k] by k-magnitude\n   - Progressive resolution expansion: add point every 140 points\n\nC. Repair mechanisms:\n   - Two-stage clipping:\n        First ensure integral: clamp values in [0,1] with weighted average\n        Then enforce \u03a3h[i]=n/2 while preserving function shape\n\nStage transitions triggered by:\n   - Lag coverage percentage (70% lag space satisfied)\n   - Wall time (automatic checkpointing)\n   - Diminishing improvement ratio (objective/x_ratio < 0.99)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nUse algorithm architecture from initial code but modify core optimization:\n\ndef main_run(seed=42):\n    global best_h, best_c5\n    cfg = {\n        'resolutions': [560, 1120],   # progressive increase\n        'max_time': budget_s,\n        'max_iter': 1000,\n        'evals_per_res': 100,\n        'lag_penalty_weights': 1.0   # adaptive by lag-magnitude\n    }\n    repair initial candidate\n    primary_candidates = generate_diverse_pop(10)\n    secondary_candidates = []\n    current_res = 560\n    for res in cfg['resolutions'] (if current_res <  \u2264 budget_s):\n        new_pop = []\n        for cand in primary_candidates:\n            new_cand = upscale(cand, res)\n            weighted_optimize(new_cand, res, lag_weights: scaling factor)\n            repair(new_cand) ensure integral\n            check_overlap(new_cand): for k in processing_range, enforce P(k*B) penalty if excessive, but with budget_s/3 greedy iteration for penalty reduction\n            record metrics\n            new_pop.append(new_cand)\n        secondary_candidates = new_pop[:5]  # select top performers\n        primary_candidates = secondary_candidates  #inherit diversity\n    # exponential weighted selection\n    return best_h, best_c5, len(best_h)\n\nConstraint handling through:\n   - Lag-specific penalties (0.1 to 10 based on lag index plotted)\n   - Progressively increasing weights as optimization proceeds\n   - Repair prioritizes integral over detailed function shape optimization\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDeploys expert-controlled variations:\n- Vertical treatment: ascent-descent bidirectional search\n- Horizontal treatment: resolution doubling\n- Diagonal treatment: multi-lag constraint maximization\nfor each resolution, 2-3 worst lags are explicitly penalized in 3-minute bursts\n\nAdaptive heuristics:\n- Lag detection: identify lag-frequency clusters\n- Penalty curve: exponential penalty at k magnitudes above 1.0\n- Progressive focus: shift to unresolved lag groups once mastery achieved\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Progressively increasing resolution coupled with decreasing time per eval\n- Early termination at face of plateau (threshold: objective improvement < 1e-6 per epoch)\n- Repair fallback: prioritize valid integral constraint over detailed shape\n- Lag-specific error recovery: subset of 20 lag constraints if invalid detected\n- Resolution doubling points set to preserve wall time budget\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEach candidate must:\n   1. Pass box constraint check (0\u2264h[i]\u22641)\n   2. Integral must be within tolerance of n/2\n   3. Evaluate overlap integral via mode=full scipy.correlate followed by dx scaling\n\nFinal solution metrics computed via:\nfinal_overlap = compute_overlap(best_h)\nreturn best_h, final_overlap, len(best_h)\n\nIncludes final consistency check:\nif sum(best_h)*(2/len(best_h)) > n/2 - 1e-4 and sum(best_h) < n/2 + 1e-4 and not penalized:\n    accept\nelse:\n    repair_high_priority(best_h)  # weights integral over shape",
